; A `;;` comment is scanned by the lexer; a `;` comment is not. Inside a `;;`
; comment, a backslash followed by anything other than a letter hangs the
; lexer forever. The same line written with a single `;` runs fine, and so
; does `;; with - dash`. Both forms are stripped from the token stream, so
; nothing here reaches the program either way.
;
; This header is deliberately written with single semicolons.

;; with \- dash
print "should print but never does"
